"""CySA+ Domain 3 drill content — objectives 3.1 to 3.3.

Original prose written against CompTIA's published CS0-003 objectives
(build/cysa-spine.json). Class decks and transcripts are read for scope
only; no instructor wording or Knowledge Check questions are reproduced,
because the repo is public.

Shapes:
    FLASHCARDS  (objective, front, back)
    QUIZ        (objective, question, [4 choices], answer_index, explanation)
    SCENARIOS   (objective, situation, question, [4 choices], answer_index, explanation)

Recovered 2026-10-25 from the published page (d3.frag.html) after the session that
built it ended without committing its source. drill_page.py regenerates that
page byte for byte from this file, which is the proof the recovery is lossless.
"""

FLASHCARDS = [
 ('3.1',
  'Name the seven stages of the Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain in order.',
  'Reconnaissance, Weaponization, Delivery, Exploitation, Installation, Command and Control, Actions on Objectives. The value is that it is sequential and linear: break any single link and the stages after it do not happen.'),
 ('3.1',
  'Which kill chain stage leaves the defender no telemetry at all, and why?',
  "Weaponization. It happens entirely on the attacker's infrastructure — pairing a payload with a deliverable artifact — and never touches the target. You can only infer it afterwards from what was delivered."),
 ('3.1',
  'What are the four vertices of the Diamond Model?',
  'Adversary, Capability, Infrastructure, Victim. Any one known vertex is a pivot toward the others: an IP address (infrastructure) found on one victim leads to other victims using the same infrastructure.'),
 ('3.1',
  'What does the Diamond Model do that the kill chain does not?',
  'It relates the elements of a single intrusion to each other rather than sequencing events. The kill chain tells you where in an attack you are; the Diamond Model tells you how the pieces connect, which is what lets you pivot from one indicator to the rest of a campaign.'),
 ('3.1',
  'What is MITRE ATT&CK, and what are its tactics and techniques?',
  "A knowledge base of real adversary behavior. Tactics are the attacker's goals, such as persistence or lateral movement; techniques are the specific ways each goal is achieved, each with an identifier. It describes what attackers actually do rather than proposing an idealized sequence."),
 ('3.1',
  'Kill chain or ATT&CK — which do you reach for, and when?',
  'The kill chain for communicating where an intrusion got to and which link to break, especially to non-specialists. ATT&CK for detection engineering and hunting, because it names specific observable behaviors you can write rules against. They answer different questions and are not competitors.'),
 ('3.1',
  'What is the OSSTMM for?',
  'A methodology for security testing that is measurable and repeatable — defining how a test is scoped and conducted so two testers produce comparable results. It covers operational security broadly, not just web applications.'),
 ('3.1',
  'What does the OWASP Testing Guide cover, and how does it differ from the OWASP Top 10?',
  'The Testing Guide is a methodology for how to test a web application, step by step. The Top 10 is an awareness list of the most critical risks. One tells you how to look; the other tells you what is commonly found.'),
 ('3.1',
  'Why does an analyst need an attack framework at all?',
  'It converts scattered observations into a structure with a next question. Without one you have a list of alerts; with one you know which stage you are seeing, therefore what preceded it, what probably follows, and where to look for the parts you have not found yet.'),
 ('3.2',
  'Name the phases of the incident response life cycle.',
  'Preparation; Detection and Analysis; Containment, Eradication and Recovery; Post-Incident Activity. The cycle is a loop, not a line — post-incident findings feed back into preparation.'),
 ('3.2',
  'What distinguishes scope from impact during an incident?',
  'Scope is how far it reaches — which hosts, accounts, data and segments are involved. Impact is what it costs — downtime, loss, regulatory exposure, reputational harm. Scope drives the technical response; impact drives who must be told and how fast.'),
 ('3.2',
  'What is the first question containment has to answer?',
  'Whether to isolate immediately or observe first. Pulling a host offline stops the damage and destroys volatile evidence along with the chance to see what else the attacker touches. That is a judgment call about which matters more in this incident, and it belongs to the incident lead.'),
 ('3.2',
  'In what order should volatile evidence be collected?',
  'Most volatile first: CPU registers and cache, then memory, then network state and running processes, then disk, then remote logging and backups, then physical media. Collecting disk before memory loses the memory, and memory is where injected code and keys live.'),
 ('3.2',
  'Why does evidence acquisition need a hash and a chain of custody?',
  'A hash taken at acquisition and re-verified later proves the image has not changed. The chain of custody records who held it and when. Without both, the evidence may be technically sound and still unusable in any proceeding that matters.'),
 ('3.2',
  'What is the difference between eradication and recovery?',
  'Eradication removes the attacker and what they left — malware, accounts, persistence, modified configuration. Recovery restores service and verifies it is clean. Declaring recovery before eradication is complete is how an incident reopens a week later.'),
 ('3.2',
  'When is re-imaging the right call rather than cleaning?',
  'Whenever the integrity of the host cannot be established — kernel-level compromise, unclear persistence, or an attacker who had administrative access. Cleaning assumes you found everything. Re-imaging from a known-good baseline does not require that assumption.'),
 ('3.2',
  'What role do compensating controls play during an incident?',
  'They hold the risk down while the real fix is impossible or not yet ready — blocking at the perimeter when a server cannot be patched mid-quarter, or forcing additional authentication on accounts that may be exposed. They are deliberate and temporary, and they need an owner and an end date.'),
 ('3.2',
  'What makes an indicator of compromise useful rather than just true?',
  'That it is specific enough to act on and durable enough to still match. A file hash is precise but changes with every rebuild; a behavior such as a service binary writing to a user profile survives recompilation. Both have a place, and knowing which you are holding decides how long it is worth hunting for.'),
 ('3.2',
  'Why is log correlation across sources worth more than any single source?',
  'Because each source sees one facet. The firewall saw the connection, the endpoint saw the process, the identity provider saw the logon. Individually each is ordinary; together they are a timeline. This is also why clock synchronization is a precondition for everything.'),
 ('3.3',
  'What belongs in an incident response plan?',
  'Who decides and who is informed, how severity is classified, escalation paths and contact details, legal and regulatory obligations with their deadlines, and the authority to disconnect or shut down. The plan exists so these are settled before anyone is under pressure.'),
 ('3.3',
  'What is a playbook, and how does it differ from the plan?',
  'The plan is the governing framework. A playbook is the specific sequence for one incident type — ransomware, phishing, data exfiltration — naming the steps, the tools and the decision points. One organization has one plan and many playbooks.'),
 ('3.3',
  'What is a tabletop exercise, and what is it actually testing?',
  'A discussion-based walkthrough of a scenario with no systems touched. It tests whether people know their roles, whether the plan survives contact with a real situation, and where the gaps and ambiguities are — which is usually communication and decision authority rather than technology.'),
 ('3.3',
  'Why does tooling belong in the preparation phase?',
  'Because the middle of an incident is the worst time to discover you have no forensic imaging capability, no out-of-band communication, or no write blockers. Preparation means the tools exist, are licensed, are reachable, and someone has used them before.'),
 ('3.3',
  'How do business continuity and disaster recovery relate to incident response?',
  'They overlap but answer different questions. Incident response deals with the adversary; BC keeps the business running; DR restores systems and data. A ransomware event invokes all three, which is why their plans have to be consistent with each other.'),
 ('3.3',
  'What is root cause analysis meant to find?',
  'The condition that allowed the incident, not the step that triggered it. "A user clicked a link" is a trigger. "Macros execute by default and nothing alerts on Office spawning a shell" is a root cause — because it is the thing that, if fixed, prevents recurrence.'),
 ('3.3',
  'What makes a lessons-learned session worth holding?',
  'That it produces owned, dated actions and is run blamelessly. Held soon enough that memory is intact, with everyone involved present, asking what happened, what worked, what did not, and what changes. Without assigned follow-ups it is a meeting, not an improvement.'),
 ('3.3',
  'What does forensic analysis contribute after an incident is contained?',
  'It establishes what actually happened rather than what was assumed — how entry was gained, what was accessed, whether data left. That determines notification obligations, supports any legal action, and tells you whether eradication was complete.'),
 ('3.3',
  'Why is post-incident communication part of the life cycle rather than an afterthought?',
  'Because regulators, customers, insurers and staff all have claims on what is known, and several of those come with legal deadlines. Deciding in the moment who says what to whom is how organizations create a second incident out of the first.'),
]

